# Literature Survey: Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Medical Guidelines

This survey reviews recent research relevant to this project's approach: using
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to answer questions grounded in clinical
practice guidelines. It covers why RAG is used in medicine, what the current
research shows about its effectiveness, the datasets it draws on, and open
problems the field hasn't solved yet.

## 1. Why RAG for medicine specifically

Large language models memorize facts during training, but that memorized
knowledge can be outdated, incomplete, or simply wrong — a serious problem in
a domain where an incorrect answer has real consequences. The core appeal of
RAG is that it separates *what the model knows how to say* from *what it says
it as fact*: instead of relying on memorized parameters, the model is handed
the actual source text at question time and asked to answer strictly from it.
This project's dataset (`epfl-llm/guidelines`) is itself part of the research
landscape — it's the same clinical guidelines corpus assembled for training
the Meditron family of open-source medical LLMs, comprising 46,000 guideline
documents alongside PubMed literature (Chen et al., 2023).

## 2. Key papers

### Grounding Large Language Models in Clinical Evidence (Lewis et al., 2025)

The closest published analogue to this project. The authors built a RAG
system over the UK's NICE clinical guidelines and evaluated it rigorously: a
hybrid retrieval setup tested against roughly ten thousand text chunks pulled
from three hundred guideline documents, checked against nearly eight thousand
queries. Retrieval quality was strong — the correct chunk ranked first over
80% of the time, and appeared somewhere in the top ten results over 99% of
the time. The generation side showed an even starker result: grounding
answers in retrieved text raised faithfulness (whether an answer is actually
supported by the source) by close to 65 percentage points for one model,
while a non-RAG baseline scored as low as roughly one-third on the same
faithfulness measure. The paper's conclusion is direct — RAG is a credible,
scalable way to deploy LLMs safely in a clinical information setting, though
the authors are careful to note that human-centered evaluation is still
needed before real clinical deployment.
[arXiv:2510.02967](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02967)

### Improving LLM Applications in Biomedicine with RAG (Liu & McCoy et al., JAMIA, 2025)

A systematic review and meta-analysis rather than a single system. The
authors screened several hundred candidate studies down to twenty that
directly compared an LLM's performance with and without RAG on biomedical
tasks, then pooled the results statistically. The combined effect favored RAG
by a clear margin (pooled odds ratio of 1.35), meaning the improvement isn't
just anecdotal to a handful of demo systems — it holds up in aggregate across
independently-run studies. The authors also proposed a set of practical
development guidelines for teams building clinical RAG systems, and flagged
integrating RAG directly into electronic health record workflows as a
priority direction for future work.
[DOI: 10.1093/jamia/ocaf008](https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaf008)

### Retrieval-Augmented Generation for LLMs in Healthcare: A Systematic Review (Amugongo et al., PLOS Digital Health, 2025)

A broader systematic review of how RAG has been applied across healthcare
generally, not limited to guideline lookup. Useful for understanding the
wider landscape this project's approach sits inside — question answering,
summarization, and clinical decision support are all active RAG application
areas, each with different retrieval and evaluation challenges.
[DOI: 10.1371/journal.pdig.0000877](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0000877)

### MEDITRON-70B: Scaling Medical Pretraining for Large Language Models (Chen et al., 2023)

Not a RAG paper, but directly relevant because it's the source of this
project's dataset. The EPFL team built open-source 7B and 70B parameter LLMs
by continuing pretraining on a curated medical corpus: peer-reviewed PubMed
literature plus a newly assembled set of roughly 46,000 clinical practice
guidelines spanning multiple countries, hospitals, and international health
organizations. This paper represents the alternative strategy to RAG — baking
domain knowledge into model weights via continued pretraining, rather than
retrieving it at question time. Comparing the two papers above is instructive:
Lewis et al. found a RAG-grounded general-purpose model outperformed a
medical-finetuned model on faithfulness, suggesting grounding may matter more
than domain-specific training for factual reliability, though the two
approaches are not mutually exclusive.
[arXiv:2311.16079](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16079)

## 3. What the research agrees on

- **RAG measurably reduces hallucination** in biomedical question-answering
  compared to an ungrounded LLM — this shows up consistently across
  individually reported case studies and in pooled meta-analysis.
- **Retrieval quality is the bottleneck.** Every paper above spends more
  effort evaluating the retriever (recall, ranking accuracy) than the
  generator — if the right chunk isn't retrieved, no amount of generation
  quality can compensate.
- **Chunking and embedding choices are non-trivial engineering decisions**,
  not implementation details — the NICE guidelines paper specifically used a
  hybrid embedding approach rather than a single dense-vector model, because
  guideline text mixes narrative prose with structured tables and dosage
  data that plain semantic embeddings handle poorly.

## 4. Open problems (and where this project sits)

- **Safety is improved, not solved.** Even well-grounded RAG systems in the
  literature don't reach zero unsafe or incorrect responses under expert
  clinical review — meaning production use still requires human oversight,
  not just a good retrieval pipeline.
- **Most published systems use larger, more curated corpora** than the small
  sample used in this project (this repo indexes ~300 guideline documents for
  learning purposes, versus ~300–46,000 in the research above at a much
  larger operational scale) — retrieval quality and topic coverage would need
  to scale up substantially for anything beyond a demo.
- **Evaluation methodology is still maturing.** Metrics like faithfulness and
  context precision (used via the RAGAS framework in several of these papers)
  are becoming standard, but there's no universal benchmark yet for "is this
  clinical RAG system safe to deploy" the way there is for, say, image
  classification accuracy.

